WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.158 --> 00:00:06.149
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.149 --> 00:00:11.378
Ramesh, a 27-year-old farmer in India

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:18.595
lives happily with this family,
with his wife and two kids.

00:00:18.595 --> 00:00:22.555
Everyday, morning, he goes to his farm,

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:27.285
works hard, grows his crops,
comes back tired and

00:00:27.285 --> 00:00:32.460
exhausted in the evening to
have a meal with his family.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.831
On one day when his wife
could not find him back home,

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:45.007
she took the kids and
went out searching for him.

00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:50.152
She found him hanging dead
on a tree above his farm.

00:00:50.152 --> 00:00:55.053
Ramesh committed suicide
because he could not repay

00:00:55.053 --> 00:01:00.740
a $200 loan that he took from
a microfinance institute.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.220
I did not know Ramesh.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.580
But as I sat in my highrise
apartment in Abu Dhabi,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:17.382
having built a career in financial
services, I felt I'd betrayed

00:01:17.382 --> 00:01:24.290
my grandfather and
generations before him who were farmers.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:31.830
I felt I'd betrayed the community and
small village in India that I come from.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:39.370
In 2006, when the Nobel Peace Prize
was given to Muhammad Yunus for

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:45.260
microfinance, I was excited and
happy because

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.510
it was considered the ultimate financial
solution that would help the poor.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:58.850
Foreign aid organizations, wealthy
philanthropists, and global corporations,

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:03.747
invested millions of
dollars into microfinance.

00:02:03.747 --> 00:02:10.130
Even us, who had a few dollars to spare,
went on the websites like Kiva, and

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:16.131
on click of a mouse,
lent money to farmers in Ecuador.

00:02:16.131 --> 00:02:23.110
Microfinance appealed to the emotions of a
lot of people because of the simple model.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:29.790
It would lend tiny loans to
the poor who could invest,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.530
grow business, see great returns,
and then climb out of poverty.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.540
Today, ten years after
the Nobel Peace Prize,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.860
40 years since microfinance has started,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.730
there's little evidence that
microfinance helps the poor.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.020
On the contrary, there are several
studies that shows it does not.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.800
A study done here at Stanford University,
across 87

00:03:01.516 --> 00:03:06.780
in Bangladesh,
found microfinance to have no impact

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.780
on the income of the poor, and in certain
cases, found it to have a negative impact.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.458
A study done at MIT,
across 52 neighborhoods in

00:03:18.458 --> 00:03:24.740
Hyderabad in India found microfinance
did not have any impact on health,

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.210
education or income of the poor people.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.750
Finally, a study done at
Yale University in Philippines

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.660
found no impact on income of
the poor due to microfinance.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:47.430
Microfinance, as it stands on a shaky
foundation due to three main reasons.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:54.050
First, it considers every poor
person to be an entrepreneur.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.590
You give him capital, he could invest and

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.019
grow a business,
which is in fact not true.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:07.150
Studies have found that less than 2% of

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:12.220
microenterprises survive for
more than three years.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.880
Number two, the high interest rates.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.890
I was speaking to a CEO of
microfinance firm recently and

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:26.480
one of the first questions I asked
him was, what's your interest rate?

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.040
Obviously, he did not give me an answer
but he explained how it works.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.180
This is what he said,
we give a loan of $10 for

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.340
two weeks and expect $12 in return.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.880
If you do the math like I did, that
works out to 20% interest for two weeks.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.160
If you compute that on an annual basis,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.190
it works to thousands of
percentage in interest.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:59.335
Thousands of percentage in interest
every poor person has to pay.

00:04:59.335 --> 00:05:03.430
Microfinance justifies this saying,

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:08.130
it's to cover the cost of operation,
it's to cover the risk of the business.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:14.257
Forbes Magazine published a list
of top 50 microfinance institutes.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:20.932
On an average, it found these firms
generating 80% return on capital,

00:05:20.932 --> 00:05:24.446
80% return.

00:05:24.446 --> 00:05:31.300
There are very few businesses in the world
which generate 80% annually on capital.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.728
And finally,
their abusive collection practices.

00:05:35.728 --> 00:05:43.117
Microfinance claims 95% collection
rate but how do they achieve this?

00:05:43.117 --> 00:05:49.230
Was a debt collector for
Grameen Bank in Bangladesh.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.320
He was asked to retire early because he
questioned their collection practices.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.511
They were asked to threaten and abuse poor
borrowers who were not able to repay.

00:06:00.511 --> 00:06:05.278
Poor borrowers were asked
to sell their clothes,

00:06:05.278 --> 00:06:08.868
or even sell their children,
to repay the loan.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:15.780
Microfinance started off with the best

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.109
of intentions but has failed to
deliver on the promise to the poor.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.370
As I did my research, even I failed to

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:31.860
find a silver bullet solution for
the problems of the poor.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.390
Problems of the poor are big,
complex and challenging.

00:06:39.848 --> 00:06:45.630
Today, Ramesh's wife faces the challenge

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:50.100
of feeding her young children
at least one meal a day.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:57.740
Millions of people across the globe
face similar challenges and

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:01.430
tough choices, each and
every day of their lives.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:08.630
What they need is not a microfinance law
that could make their life difficult.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:14.990
What they need is a regular source of
income that can make their life easier.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:22.990
I am in quest for
searching for a solution and

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:29.434
I need your help and
support to find the solution.

00:07:29.434 --> 00:07:33.668
I've launched this website which is

00:07:33.668 --> 00:07:38.857
ipledgeworld.com to source solutions that

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:43.780
could help solve the problems of the poor.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.034
Let us take this first step together,

00:07:50.034 --> 00:07:54.225
to change the lives of the poor people,

00:07:54.225 --> 00:08:00.321
to change the way organizations
interact with the poor,

00:08:00.321 --> 00:08:07.308
and to dream of a changed world
that would have a better tomorrow.

00:08:07.308 --> 00:08:08.562
Thank you very much.

00:08:08.562 --> 00:08:14.785
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:19.669
[MUSIC]

